The volume of a cone $V = \frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h$
$\therefore h = \frac{3V}{\pi r^2}$
$\therefore h \propto \frac{1}{r^2}$ {as $V$ is a constant}

If the height of the cone is decreased to $10.8\text{ cm}$, then $h$ is multiplied by $\frac{10.8}{14.3} = \frac{108}{143}$.
$\therefore r^2$ is multiplied by $\frac{143}{108}$ {as $h \propto \frac{1}{r^2}$}
$\therefore r$ is multiplied by $\sqrt{\frac{143}{108}}$ {as $r > 0$}
So, the radius of the cone is increased to $2.8 \times \sqrt{\frac{143}{108}} \approx 3.22\text{ cm}$.
